    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Glioblastoma (GBM) is the most malignant primary brain tumor in adults, with a median survival time of one and a half years. Traditional treatments including radiation, chemotherapy and surgery are not curative, and it is crucial to explore novel targets for therapeutic treatment. Gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT) is a family of enzymes involved in the metabolism of glutathione, the cell’s main water-soluble antioxidant. GGT7 is a novel GGT family member whose function has not been fully determined and has not been previously studied in cancer. Recent reports have demonstrated that GBM patients have decreased expression of GGT7 but not GGT1 or GGT5, suggesting that loss of GGT7 may enhance glial tumor growth. We hypothesize that GGT7 could be acting as a tumor suppressor in GBM due to its role in pro-oxidation and detoxification. In this thesis project we sought out to determine if GGT7 plays a role in GBM tumorigenesis. To determine if GGT7 is involved in GBM tumorigenesis we transduced GBM cell lines with GGT7. We determined that an increase in GGT7 resulted in a minimal decrease in proliferation and 3-fold reduction in anchorage independent growth at normal serum conditions. Surprisingly, we observed a more pronounced reduction in GBM growth under starvation conditions, yielding a 2-fold decrease in proliferation and 3-fold decrease in anchorage independent growth. This suggests that when GBM cells undergo stressful situations GGT7 reduces tumorigenic phenotypes. Correspondingly, we reduced expression of GGT7 using both siRNAs and shRNA.  We found that reducing GGT7 expression increased proliferation in the GBM cells. This was seen at both normal (1.5-fold increase) and low serum conditions (2-fold increase), with a greater increase at low serum conditions. GGT7 expression also modulated the activity of reactive oxygen species (ROS) in the cell. ROS levels were decreased by 14-15% at normal serum conditions and 11-33% at low serum conditions. Together, our study suggests that GGT7 may act as a tumor suppressor in glial tumorigenesis by protecting the cells from ROS damage and decreasing proliferation under stressful, low nutrient conditions.  Further analysis will be conducted to determine if GGT7 can be used as a biomarker or therapeutic target for GBM.",
